  "releaseNotes": "Sharp photos: the capture no longer waits. The frame is drawn to the canvas synchronously the moment\n\t\t\ttakePhoto is called — the very frame the user was looking at when they pressed — instead of waiting two\n\t\t\tvideo frames first, and the .NET side no longer yields to the renderer before firing. The screen flash\n\t\t\t(and the wait it needs) now happens only when the flash is actually armed, where before every single\n\t\t\tphoto paid for it. Streams also ask for a frame rate floor, which is really an exposure ceiling: without\n\t\t\tit the auto exposure stretched past 1/10 s indoors and hand shake was baked into the pixels.\n\n\t\t\tTap to focus: tapping the viewfinder focuses and meters the exposure at that point, marked with a frame,\n\t\t\tlike the system camera. Beyond framing, this is what removes the shutter lag — a capture no longer has to\n\t\t\tconverge focus and exposure from scratch. Custom ICameraCapture implementations gain two members\n\t\t\t(AttachFocusAsync, DetachFocusAsync); returning false from the first keeps the previous behaviour, and\n\t\t\tEnableTapToFocus switches the whole thing off.\n\n\t\t\tSelf timer: the shutter can now wait before it fires. TimerOptions is the whole switch — pass no presets\n\t\t\tand there is no timer button at all, exactly like the zoom and quality bars. With a delay armed the\n\t\t\tshutter starts a countdown, shown big over the viewfinder, and becomes the cancel button; the capture\n\t\t\titself still goes through the same path as an immediate shot (OnBeforeCapture is evaluated when the\n\t\t\tshutter really fires, not when the countdown is armed), and the countdown is dropped on close, on a\n\t\t\tcamera switch and on dispose. Nothing is asked of ICameraCapture, so custom backends get it for free.\n\t\t\tThe top row now groups the timer and the torch on the right, so it no longer rearranges itself when\n\t\t\tthere is no quality bar.\n\n\t\t\tPinch to zoom: two fingers over the viewfinder now move the zoom continuously, from the minimum of the active lens up to the maximum the device can really do, with a floating indicator of the current factor. The presets stay as shortcuts. Native zoom is used first and only the remainder is cropped, so going past the camera's own maximum no longer throws away the zoom it could do. Custom ICameraCapture implementations gain three members (AttachPinchAsync, UpdatePinchConfigAsync, DetachPinchAsync); returning false from the first keeps the previous behaviour.",
